; expect 6
; A bare constructor name in pattern position is REFUSED since
; 2026-08-17. This fixture used to pin the opposite - stage0's rule,
; whose parser makes every bare identifier a variable pattern, so the
; first arm matched everything - and it pinned a wrong answer to do it:
; `(f (Cons2 1 (Nil2)))` expected 5, which is the `Nil2` arm, for a
; value that is a `Cons2`.
;
; That is not a type error and it never was; `Nil2` is a perfectly
; well-typed binder. It is the other thing this project refuses on
; sight - a silent wrong answer, indistinguishable from a real one
; (docs/memory-model.md MM-VAL-9b) - and it is reachable by writing
; what every ML-family language in the reader's background writes.
; The built-in `Option` guarantees a new user meets it early:
; `(match o (None 0) ((Some x) x))` always takes the first arm.
;
; The refusal is AX3008 with a machine-applicable fix rewriting the
; span from `Nil2` to `(Nil2)`, which is safe to auto-apply because
; both are patterns the grammar accepts. A lowercase binder is
; unaffected: the test is whether the spelling resolves to a
; constructor, not what case it is in.
;
; `tests/diagnostics/562-bare-ctor-pattern.ax` pins the diagnostic.
; This case now pins the BEHAVIOUR of the parenthesised form, which is
; what the old comment already said was the only spelling that tests
; the tag.
(data L2 (Nil2) (Cons2 Int L2))

(:: f (-> L2 Int))
(fn (f l)
  (match l
    ((Nil2) 5)
    ((Cons2 h t) 6)))

(:: main Int)
(fn (main) (f (Cons2 1 (Nil2))))
